pa-ai
=====

Origin
------
This crate is a modified fork of the `pa-ai` crate from
PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent (https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent),
the Rust side of the Prime Agent product. It is a Rust port of that product's
TypeScript `packages/ai` tree (provider clients, model registry, streaming,
usage accounting) and the model-policy engine of its TypeScript
`packages/catalog/src/compat/*` tree.

Upstream license: MIT, Copyright (c) 2025-2026 Prime Intellect Ltd. The
upstream license text is vendored at `vendor/LICENSE-prime-agent`; it also
credits the original TypeScript product, "a port of the Prime Agent TypeScript
product, originally Copyright (c) 2025 Mario Zechner". That MIT license and
copyright notice apply to this crate.

Its only first-party (workspace) dependency is `pa-types`, one-way; wire/domain
types live there and are re-exported from `src/types.rs`. Everything else in
`Cargo.toml` is a crates.io dependency.

Modifications by oh-my-tau
--------------------------
This copy has been changed. It is NOT byte-identical to upstream, and files
carrying the upstream header are not verbatim upstream content. Substantive
changes:

* Compat engine (`src/compat/`): a port of the TS model-policy compat engine
  (`packages/catalog/src/compat/*`). `src/compat/resolve.rs` is the single
  resolver (`resolve_model_policy`) layering per-API defaults, host-derived
  flags, compat-cascade axes, and sparse spec overrides; it preserves the TS
  distinction between a declared-`undefined` key and an undeclared key.
  Supporting modules: `cascade.rs` (rule-tree axis resolution),
  `hosts.rs` (endpoint host classification and Anthropic endpoint-shape
  predicates, marker matching via `regex`), `revision.rs` (identifier revision
  triples), `taxonomy.rs`, `model.rs` (wire `Model` <-> resolver glue and
  thinking-metadata materialization), `view.rs`, `mod.rs`.
* Generated compat rule data (`data/compat/rules.json`, `data/compat/axes.json`,
  ~446 KB combined) is compiled into the crate with `include_str!` and parsed
  into `OnceLock` singletons by `src/compat/mod.rs`. The data is generated from
  the TS tree by `scripts/compat-oracle/gen-data.ts` (repo root), not hand
  edited; the axes table carries the TS `AXES` wire/request keys and
  `API_COMPAT_RECORDS`.
* Compat oracle test (`src/compat/oracle.rs`, test-only): asserts the Rust
  resolver reproduces TS `resolveModelPolicy` for every (provider, model) in a
  dump of the local model database plus the bundled catalog. Fixture lookup
  honors `$OMP_COMPAT_ORACLE_DIR`, then `<repo>/target/compat-oracle/`, then the
  legacy `<repo>/runs/omp-rust/compat-oracle/`; fixtures are produced by
  `scripts/compat-oracle/oracle.ts` and are not distributed in this repository.
* Compiled fallback catalog (`src/models_generated.rs`, ~4.6k lines): the
  runtime catalog chain is validated disk cache -> packaged bundled snapshot ->
  this compiled table. It is a hand-maintained port of the TS
  `packages/ai/src/models.generated.ts` `MODELS` layout (same provider/model
  ordering, `thinkingLevelMap` pairs, and the `compat` subset the TS fallback
  file carries); there is no generator, so refreshes are manual.
* Bedrock transport (`src/providers/bedrock/h2.rs`, `eventstream.rs`,
  `goaway.rs`, `auth.rs`, `src/utils_inner/h2_classify.rs`): the default
  transport is h2c prior-knowledge HTTP/2 over cleartext, mirroring the TS
  client's `NodeHttp2Handler` (isolated session per request, no transport
  retries or timeout), with `H2TlsAlpn` for https endpoints and an http1
  fallback selected by `AWS_BEDROCK_FORCE_HTTP1=1` or a configured proxy.
  Failure texts are pinned to the TS strings and classified through
  `utils_inner::h2_classify`. This required adding `h2` (with `stream`) and
  `bytes` to `Cargo.toml`, and enabling the `http2` feature on the shared
  `reqwest` client while every non-bedrock provider stays `http1_only()`.
* Provider surface: providers beyond the upstream set, including `devin`
  (model-router/agent wire), `kimi` (device-identity common headers),
  `google_gemini_cli`, `openrouter`, `systemone` (structured-decision
  protocol), `azure_openai_responses`, `openai_codex_responses` (WebSocket
  session, exposed as `codex_debug`), plus the `faux` test provider and
  `simple_options` token-ceiling helpers.
* OAuth flows (`src/oauth/`): Anthropic callback, GitHub Copilot, Kimi,
  OpenAI Codex, xAI, shared PKCE and provider-http plumbing; `src/env_api_keys.rs`
  resolves credentials from the environment.
* Diagnostics and utilities (`src/utils_inner/`, re-exported as
  `pa_ai::utils`): provider-error shapes with per-SDK user-facing texts and
  diagnostic names, connection-error profiles, stream-failure classification
  and retry, overflow detection, JSON repair/streaming parsing, SSE decoding,
  unicode sanitization, and an HTTP inspector.
* Parity and differential test harnesses: `src/transport_parity_tests.rs`,
  `src/prime_inference_differential_test.rs`, per-provider `*_tests.rs`
  (e.g. `providers/anthropic/stream_tests.rs`,
  `providers/openai_completions/body_parity_tests.rs`), and
  `tests/differential/` (a Python replay server plus a TS driver used to diff
  Rust output against the TypeScript implementation). The differential
  fixtures under `tests/testdata/` are captures of provider wire traffic.
* Crate documentation (`src/lib.rs`, `README.md`) reflects this fork's surface
  (e.g. the `codex_debug` module, `kimi_common_headers`, the `utils` facade),
  and `Cargo.toml` declares workspace-inherited version/edition/license/lints
  plus the dependencies the ports above required.

Nothing in this crate is relicensed: the fork remains under the upstream MIT
terms reproduced in `vendor/LICENSE-prime-agent`.
